2026-05-22 08:17:51,323 - INFO - Response: The Watergate scandal had a significant impact on American politics, leading to the resignation of President Richard Nixon in 1974. The scandal began when members of the Nixon administration were caught in a series of illegal activities, including the use of government funds to cover up the break-in at the Democratic National Committee headquarters in the Watergate complex. The scandal led to a series of investigations by the House Judiciary Committee and the Senate Select Committee on Presidential Campaign Activities, which ultimately resulted in the impeachment of Nixon.
The Watergate scandal had a number of effects on American politics. It led to increased scrutiny of the executive branch and the role of the presidency, as well as a greater emphasis on transparency and accountability in government. It also led to the creation of the Federal Election Commission, which was responsible for regulating campaign finance and ensuring that elections were fair and free from corruption. Additionally, the scandal helped to establish the precedent of the "impeachment" process, which allows the House of Representatives to impeach a federal official and remove them from office if they are found guilty of certain crimes.
Overall, the Watergate scandal had a significant impact on American politics, shaping the way that government operates and the role of the presidency in particular.
